WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.823 --> 00:00:05.525
CNC means that a computer drives the machine’s motors, following a program.

2
00:00:06.244 --> 00:00:08.799
This is such a machine, a machining centre.

3
00:00:09.811 --> 00:00:11.704
The spindle is the spinning shaft.

4
00:00:12.471 --> 00:00:15.200
On a lathe it holds the part, on a mill the tool.

5
00:00:15.890 --> 00:00:19.245
A motor of typically 10 to 40 kilowatts drives it.

6
00:00:20.587 --> 00:00:24.535
On a lathe, X sets the diameter and Z runs along the spindle.

7
00:00:25.537 --> 00:00:30.297
On a mill, X and Y move the table, and Z moves the spindle up and down.

8
00:00:31.372 --> 00:00:35.273
A fourth and fifth axis rotate the part or the spindle head.

9
00:00:36.612 --> 00:00:38.737
The program itself is G-code.

10
00:00:39.576 --> 00:00:41.561
That is a list of short commands.

11
00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:46.355
G00 is a rapid move, fast and not cutting.

12
00:00:47.041 --> 00:00:49.119
G01 is a straight cut.

13
00:00:49.876 --> 00:00:52.906
G02 and G03 are arcs.

14
00:00:53.904 --> 00:00:55.994
M03 starts the spindle.

15
00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:00.191
M08 switches on the coolant, the cooling liquid.

16
00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:03.031
M06 changes the tool.

17
00:01:04.018 --> 00:01:07.199
F sets the feed, how fast the tool advances.

18
00:01:07.934 --> 00:01:09.467
S is the spindle speed.

19
00:01:10.219 --> 00:01:11.159
T is the tool.

20
00:01:12.467 --> 00:01:15.323
Most programs are written in CAM software.

21
00:01:16.059 --> 00:01:19.414
It turns the 3D model of the part into G-code.

22
00:01:20.498 --> 00:01:24.202
That G-code goes to the control, the computer on the machine.

23
00:01:24.905 --> 00:01:27.889
Well-known makes are Fanuc, Siemens and Heidenhain.

24
00:01:29.246 --> 00:01:30.709
Now the zero points.

25
00:01:31.435 --> 00:01:34.895
Machine zero is a fixed home position of the machine.

26
00:01:35.895 --> 00:01:38.786
A setter starts a job of 200 flanges.

27
00:01:39.488 --> 00:01:43.064
He sets G54 by touching the jaw of the vice.

28
00:01:43.790 --> 00:01:45.961
A vice is a clamp with two jaws.

29
00:01:46.692 --> 00:01:49.478
The probe is a measuring pin held in the spindle.

30
00:01:50.472 --> 00:01:55.209
The work offset G54 tells the machine where the part sits on the table.

31
00:01:56.547 --> 00:01:58.997
Every tool sticks out a different amount.

32
00:01:59.753 --> 00:02:03.050
So a tool offset stores the length and the radius of a tool.

33
00:02:04.032 --> 00:02:07.468
The setter measures tool 5, a drill, on the tool setter.

34
00:02:08.336 --> 00:02:12.202
It is 121.43 millimetres long.

35
00:02:13.197 --> 00:02:16.053
The setter measures these numbers and types them in.

36
00:02:16.748 --> 00:02:18.675
Together they are called the offsets.

37
00:02:19.707 --> 00:02:23.701
Most crashes come from wrong offsets, not from wrong programs.

38
00:02:24.426 --> 00:02:27.177
In a crash the tool hits the part or the clamps.

39
00:02:27.892 --> 00:02:31.537
Most scrapped first parts also come from wrong offsets.

40
00:02:32.809 --> 00:02:38.834
But for tool 5 he types a length of 112.43 millimetres in the table,

41
00:02:38.997 --> 00:02:42.004
instead of 121.43.

42
00:02:42.967 --> 00:02:46.833
The machine thinks the drill is 9 millimetres shorter than it is.

43
00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:49.819
So it drills 9 millimetres deeper.

44
00:02:51.156 --> 00:02:55.753
Before the first cut, a careful setter runs a new program in single block.

45
00:02:56.507 --> 00:02:58.632
That is one line per button press.

46
00:02:59.712 --> 00:03:01.430
Rapid moves are slowed down.

47
00:03:02.126 --> 00:03:04.274
And a hand rests on the stop button.

48
00:03:05.690 --> 00:03:07.373
Then comes the first-off check.

49
00:03:08.114 --> 00:03:13.037
After every setup, program change or tool change you measure the first part fully,

50
00:03:13.199 --> 00:03:14.488
before the batch runs.

51
00:03:15.505 --> 00:03:17.990
Many shops also check the last part.

52
00:03:18.783 --> 00:03:23.648
It costs 10 to 20 minutes and can save a batch of 200 wrong parts.

53
00:03:24.645 --> 00:03:29.544
In the example the first-off check finds a hole that is 9 millimetres too deep.

54
00:03:30.318 --> 00:03:33.023
One part is scrapped, 35 euros.

55
00:03:34.062 --> 00:03:40.018
Without that check it is 200 times 35 euros, so 7,000 euros of scrap.

56
00:03:41.021 --> 00:03:43.726
If the hole should stop just above a thin floor,

57
00:03:43.865 --> 00:03:46.593
a drill may break and the fixture be damaged.

58
00:03:47.320 --> 00:03:49.387
That is the holder made for this part.

59
00:03:50.783 --> 00:03:53.929
What engineers argue about are edits at the machine.

60
00:03:54.668 --> 00:03:57.176
A setter fixes a program on the control.

61
00:03:58.175 --> 00:04:01.310
If the fix never goes back into the CAM file,

62
00:04:01.449 --> 00:04:04.375
the old error returns at the next repeat order.

63
00:04:05.375 --> 00:04:06.431
Good questions.

64
00:04:07.162 --> 00:04:11.075
Is this program proven, meaning it has run before without problems?

65
00:04:11.771 --> 00:04:13.094
Where is the master version?

66
00:04:14.103 --> 00:04:17.517
How many crashes were there last year, and what did they cost?

67
00:04:18.594 --> 00:04:23.470
A spindle crash can cost 5,000 to 30,000 euros in repairs.

68
00:04:24.185 --> 00:04:26.507
On top come days to weeks of downtime.
